course: euphemisms
lesson: 9
title: Soft words for hard things: age and looks and weight
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: People are sensitive about how they look: wrinkles, baldness, being short or not pretty, or getting forgetful with age.
explain: Kind words soften the truth: wrinkles become "laughter lines" and a balding man is "thinning on top".
explain: Jokes do it too, but jokes can sting: "a face for radio" says someone is ugly, because on radio nobody sees your face.
explain: Vivid example: a friend says the new boy "has a nice personality" — that sounds like praise, but often it is a sly way to say he is not good-looking.
explain: Shops and officials use soft words as well: "plus-size" clothes, "petite" sizes, "assisted living" and "older adults".
fact: "Well-preserved" is a cheeky way to say someone is old but still looks good.
fact: "Young at heart" means someone is old but lively and full of fun.
fact: "A senior moment" is a joking name for a moment of forgetting, blamed on getting old.
fact: "A retirement community" is a housing estate built for old people.
fact: "Assisted living" is an American name for a care home where old people get help with daily life.
fact: "Laughter lines" is a kind name for wrinkles around the eyes and mouth.
fact: "Thinning on top" is a polite way to say going bald.
fact: "Follically challenged" is a joking way to say bald; follicles are the tiny holes hairs grow from.
fact: "Vertically challenged" is a joking way to say short.
fact: "Homely" in American English means plain or not pretty; in British English it means cosy and home-like.
fact: "Plain" said about a person's looks means not attractive.
fact: "A face for radio" is a joking, unkind way to say someone is ugly.
fact: "Has a nice personality" is often a sly way to say someone is not attractive.
fact: "Distinguished" said of an older man often means grey-haired or older-looking, in a good way.
fact: "Had some work done" means had cosmetic surgery, operations on the face or body that change how a person looks.
fact: "Past his prime" means he is no longer young or at his best.
fact: "Petite" means short and small, said of a woman or a clothing size.
fact: "Older adult" is the official term for an old person.
fact: "Full-figured" is a polite way to say a woman is fat or large.
fact: "Plus-size" means large clothing sizes, for people who are fat.
exercise: "Your gran is very well-preserved." What does it mean? | She is old but still looks good | She keeps a lot of jam | She is kept in a museum | She never goes outside
exercise: Grandpa still skateboards at 75. He is "young at heart". What does that mean? | He is old but lively | He has a young person's heart | He is really only 25 | He has a heart problem
exercise: Mum puts her keys in the fridge and says "Oops, senior moment." What happened? | She forgot something | She had a birthday | She got a promotion | She felt dizzy
exercise: What is a "retirement community"? | Housing built for old people | A club for people who quit | A school for teachers | A holiday camp for workers
exercise: In the US, what is "assisted living"? | A care home where old people get help | A paid job as a helper in someone's home | Living with roommates | A home for students
exercise: What are "laughter lines"? | Wrinkles around the eyes and mouth | Jokes in a comedy show | Lines of people laughing | Lines drawn on a smiley face
exercise: Uncle Pete is "thinning on top". What is happening? | He is going bald | He is losing weight | He is getting taller | His roof is leaking
exercise: A comedian calls himself "follically challenged". What does he mean? | He is bald | He is bad at following rules | He finds puzzles hard | He is very hairy
exercise: A short guy jokes "I'm vertically challenged." What does he mean? | He is short | He is scared of heights | He cannot climb stairs | He is bad at standing up
exercise: In American English, what does it mean if someone is "homely"? | Plain or not pretty | Cosy and welcoming | Always staying home | Good at housework
exercise: A story says "She was a plain girl." What does that tell you? | She was not attractive | She lived on a plain | She was very honest | She wore white clothes
exercise: Someone says a man "has a face for radio". What do they mean? | He is ugly | He has a great voice | He works on radio | He looks good on TV
exercise: "He's got a nice personality..." said with a smile and a pause. What is often meant? | He is not good-looking | He is very funny | He is the best in the class | He is very rich
exercise: A newspaper calls an older actor "distinguished". What does it usually mean? | Grey-haired and older-looking, in a good way | Famous for winning lots of big film prizes | Dressed in a uniform | Very rude and proud
exercise: Gossip says the singer "has had some work done". What does it mean? | She had cosmetic surgery | She got a new job | She finished her homework | She had her house repaired
exercise: "The boxer is past his prime." What does it mean? | He is no longer young or at his best | He is the best boxer there has ever been | He has won first prize | He forgot his gloves
exercise: A shop has a "petite" section. Who are those clothes for? | Short, small women | Pet owners | Babies | Tall women
exercise: A health report talks about "older adults". Who are they? | Old people | Teenagers | Big adults | Parents
exercise: An advert says "Swimsuits for the full-figured woman." Who are they for? | Larger women | Women who are full after dinner | Tall thin women | Women who love sport
exercise: What are "plus-size" clothes? | Clothes in large sizes | Clothes with extra pockets | Clothes for maths teachers | Clothes for winter
test: Gossip: "Look at her face — not a single wrinkle at 60. She's definitely had some work done." What does the gossip think? | She had cosmetic surgery | She works very hard | She does a lot of exercise | She sleeps a lot
test: Granny laughs: "I've got laughter lines — from 70 years of laughing at your grandad." What is she talking about? | Her wrinkles | Lines in a diary | A comedy show | Her hair parting
test: Bro: "dad's going a bit thin on top lol, nearly a cue ball." What's happening to Dad? | He is going bald | He is getting thin | He plays snooker | He fell on his head
test: Nan: "Where did I put my glasses? Senior moment!" — they are on her head. What is Nan joking about? | Her forgetfulness at her age | Her new glasses | Being the oldest person | A special moment at a party
test: A sports reporter: "At 38, the striker is clearly past his prime." What does the reporter think? | The striker is no longer at his best | The striker is the best now | The striker has just started | The striker is too young
test: A letter: "Your grandmother has moved into assisted living." Where has she moved? | A care home where she gets help | A shared flat with her old friends | A hospital for one night | A holiday home
test: Kid: "he's like 4 foot nothing, vertically challenged innit" — what is the kid saying? | The boy is short | The boy cannot climb | The boy is bad at maths | The boy is very tall
test: A mean joke: "With that mug he's got a face for radio." What is the joke saying? | He is ugly | He drinks a lot of tea | He talks a lot | He should be a singer
test: An American book: "The homely girl at the dance had no partner." What does "homely" say here? | She was plain-looking | She was cosy and kind | She stayed at home | She was the host
test: A Brit says "What a homely kitchen!" Is this rude? | No, in Britain it means cosy | Yes, it means ugly | Yes, it means dirty | No, it means very big
test: A TV ad: "Sunny Pines – a friendly retirement community." Who will live there? | Old people | Young families | Students | Holidaying workers
test: Sara reckons "plus-size" means clothes that are extra cheap. Is she right? | No, it means large sizes | Yes, they are cheap | Yes, they have extra pockets | No, it means small sizes
test: A magazine: "The distinguished gentleman with silver hair." What does "distinguished" suggest? | He looks older in an elegant way | He is a famous scientist with many prizes | He is a soldier | He is very loud
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the expression "follically challenged". | Bald | Unable to follow instructions | Possessing excessive hair | Experiencing intellectual difficulty
exam: It is given that a resident of a United States facility is described as being in "assisted living". Ascertain the nature of the facility. | A residence in which elderly persons receive help with daily tasks | A shared dwelling for students attending a nearby university | A hospital ward for acute illness | A hostel for travelling workers
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the phrase "a senior moment". | A lapse of memory attributed to advancing age | An occasion on which the eldest person speaks | A ceremony marking retirement | A period of seniority at work
exam: Whereas an American text describes a person as "homely", determine which of the following most accurately characterises that person. | Plain or unattractive in appearance | Fond of remaining at home in the evenings | Warm and hospitable | Skilled in domestic tasks
